Bug: A file that ends inside a macro call stops the server

`int j = F(1` last in a file, with `#define F(x) x`, ends in a segmentation fault
(exit code -11). valgrind puts it in getActualMacroArguments() (src/yylex.c), at
`log_debug("getActualMacroArgument: %s", lexemEnumNames[lexem])`: at the end of the
file lexem is END_OF_FILE_EXCEPTION (-2), so the table is read out of range. The
arguments are evaluated whatever the log level.

Skipping the lookup for an exception (`(int)lexem < 0`, LexemCode is unsigned) gets
past it, but then the server does not answer within 60 seconds, so something loops on
the end of the file after that. Not followed further. The commented-out
ON_LEXEM_EXCEPTION_GOTO just above would go to endOfFile, which returns NULL, and
its own comment says that will probably crash the caller.

`F(` last in a file (after.c) has not been tried on its own.

When it is fixed, the two `warningMessage(..."unterminated macro call")` calls in
getActualMacroArgument() and getActualMacroArguments() can become
parsingMessageAt(macroPosition, ...) and be asserted here. The one in
expandMacroCall() fires when a file ends right after the name of a function-like
macro, before any '(', which is no unterminated call, so it should probably go.
